item.detail:
**What happens:** `GetPt9InterlinearData_SelectorWithRepeatsAndReverseOrder_ReadsEachOnceInScanOrder` at `ParatextProjectDataProviderPt9InterlinearTests.cs:1417` seeds exactly two book files and names both in the selector, so `selected` is trivially `whole`, and the single assertion at `:1434-1436` compares two derived sequences. Mutating `SelectRequestedPt9Files` to `return scannedPaths;` - every selected read silently serving the whole project - leaves this test green. The de-duplication half does hold: returning `requestedPaths.ToList()` is caught. There is also no positive control, so an empty-versus-empty comparison would pass.

**Why it matters:** "the selection was a no-op" is the strongest failure this test could catch and the one it misses, and it is the failure the ADR calls out - a per-file importer assembling one whole copy per read and never being told. The no-op mutation is caught elsewhere in the fixture, by `GetPt9InterlinearData_Selector_StillServesSettingsDerivedParts` and `GetPt9InterlinearData_SelectorOnOverSizeProject_ServesTheSelectedFile`, so the suite is not blind to it - this test simply provides none of that protection while its name says it does.

**Fix:** Seed a third book file the selector does not name (for example `WriteProjectFile("Interlinear_fr.xml", InterlinearEsMatXml);`), drop the now-unused `whole` variable, and replace the assertion with a pinned two-element list in scan order:

```csharp
Assert.That(
    selected.Books.Select(book => book.FilePath),
    Is.EqualTo(
        new[]
        {
            "Interlinear_en/Interlinear_en_JAS.xml",
            "Interlinear_es/Interlinear_es_MAT.xml",
        }
    )
);
```

Write it multi-line as shown; csharpier reformats a single-line array literal here.

